      correctIndex: 0,
      explanation: `<div style="border-radius:10px; box-shadow:0 4px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; overflow:hidden"><div style="background-color:#01506e; border-radius:8px 8px 0 0; color:white; padding:20px"><h1 style="color:white; margin:0">Earnings Per Share (EPS) Calculation</h1></div><div style="background-color:white; padding:20px"><div style="background-color:#f9f9f9; border-left:5px solid #0099d4; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Correct Answer</h3><p style="margin:0"><span style="color:#4caf50; font-weight:bold">✓</span> <span style="color:#4caf50; font-weight:bold">Added back to net income for diluted earnings per share, and ignored for basic earnings per share.</span></p></div><div style="background-color:#f0f7fa; border-left:5px solid #68d3a7; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Explanation</h3><p style="margin:0">When calculating earnings per share (EPS) for companies with convertible debt, the treatment of interest expense differs between basic and diluted EPS calculations:</p></div><div style="background-color:#f9f9f9; border-left:5px solid #0099d4; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Basic EPS Calculation</h3><p style="margin:0">For basic EPS, interest expense on convertible debt is not adjusted. The calculation uses the actual interest expense incurred during the period. This represents the current capital structure without considering potential conversions.</p></div><div style="background-color:#f5f9fc; border-left:5px solid #207bb5; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Diluted EPS Calculation</h3><p style="margin:0">For diluted EPS, we assume conversion of the convertible debt to common stock. Since this hypothetical conversion would eliminate the related interest expense, we add back the interest expense (net of tax) to net income. This adjustment reflects what earnings would be if the debt had been converted to equity at the beginning of the period.</p></div><div style="background-color:#eef9f2; border-left:5px solid #68d3a7; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Example Calculation</h3><ul style="margin:0 0 12px 0; padding-left:22px"><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Scenario</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Company XYZ has the following financial information</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">• Net income: $1,000,000</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">• Convertible debt interest expense: $100,000</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">• Tax rate: 30%</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">• Shares outstanding: 500,000</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">• Additional shares if debt is converted: 50,000</li></ul><table cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; margin-top:15px; width:100%"><thead><tr><th style="border-bottom:2px solid #01506e; padding:10px; text-align:left">Calculation</th><th style="border-bottom:2px solid #01506e; padding:10px; text-align:left">Basic EPS</th><th style="border-bottom:2px solid #01506e; padding:10px; text-align:left">Diluted EPS</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Net Income</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$1,000,000</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$1,000,000</td></tr><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Add back: Interest expense (net of tax)</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$0</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$70,000</td></tr><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Adjusted Net Income</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$1,000,000</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$1,070,000</td></tr><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Shares Outstanding</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">500,000</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">550,000</td></tr><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Earnings Per Share</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$2.00</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$1.95</td></tr></tbody></table><div style="background-color:white; border:1px solid #dddddd; border-radius:5px; font-family:monospace; margin:0 0 12px 0; padding:12px; text-align:center"><strong>Note: Interest expense net of tax = $100,000 × (1-0.30) = $70,000. This amount is added back for diluted EPS calculation only.</strong></div></div><div style="background-color:#f0f7fa; border-left:5px solid #68d3a7; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Accounting Logic</h3><p style="margin:0">The accounting logic behind this treatment relates to how convertible debt would affect the company in a conversion scenario:</p><ul style="margin:0 0 12px 0; padding-left:22px"><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">If Conversion Occurs</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">The debt liability is eliminated</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Interest expense would no longer be incurred</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">More shares would be outstanding</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Diluted EPS Reflects</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Higher net income (due to adding back interest)</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Higher share count (due to potential new shares)</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">The &quot;what if&quot; scenario of conversion</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Journal Entry (Upon Actual Conversion)</li></ul><p style="margin:0">Although not directly related to EPS calculations, understanding the journal entry for an actual conversion helps clarify the concept:</p></div><div style="background-color:#01506e; border-radius:0 0 8px 8px; color:white; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:white; margin:0 0 12px 0">Summary</h3><ul style="margin:0 0 12px 0; padding-left:22px"><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Basic EPS: Uses actual historical structure - interest expense remains as reported</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Diluted EPS: Assumes conversion - add back interest expense (net of tax) to net income</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Purpose: Diluted EPS shows the potential impact of all dilutive securities on current shareholders</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Requirement: Companies must report both basic and diluted EPS if they have complex capital structures</li></ul></div></div></div>`,
