      explanation: `<div style="border-radius:10px; box-shadow:0 4px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; overflow:hidden"><div style="background-color:#01506e; border-radius:8px 8px 0 0; color:white; padding:20px"><h1 style="color:white; margin:0">Anti-Dilutive Securities in Diluted EPS Calculations</h1></div><div style="background-color:white; padding:20px"><div style="background-color:#f9f9f9; border-left:5px solid #0099d4; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Correct Answer</h3><p style="margin:0"><span style="color:#4caf50; font-weight:bold">✓</span> <span style="color:#4caf50; font-weight:bold">They increase EPS or have no impact on it.</span></p></div><div style="background-color:white; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Analysis of Answer Options</h3><table cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; margin-top:15px; width:100%"><thead><tr><th style="border-bottom:2px solid #01506e; padding:10px; text-align:left">Option</th><th style="border-bottom:2px solid #01506e; padding:10px; text-align:left">Analysis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Basic EPS</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$1,000,000 ÷ 100,000 shares</td></tr><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">If Preferred Converted</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">($1,000,000 + $200,000) ÷ 110,000 shares</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div style="background-color:white; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Analysis of Answer Options</h3><table cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; margin-top:15px; width:100%"><thead><tr><th style="border-bottom:2px solid #01506e; padding:10px; text-align:left">Option</th><th style="border-bottom:2px solid #01506e; padding:10px; text-align:left">Analysis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">A. They decrease net income.</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Incorrect because anti-dilutive securities typically do not directly affect net income in the context of EPS calculations. In fact, some convertible securities (like preferred stock) might increase available income to common shareholders if converted.</td></tr><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">B. They increase the weighted average number of shares outstanding.</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Incorrect because while anti-dilutive securities would indeed increase the share count if converted, this alone is not why they are excluded. They are excluded because the combined effect (on both numerator and denominator) would increase EPS.</td></tr><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">C. They have no impact on the number of shares or net income.</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Incorrect because anti-dilutive securities would impact the number of shares if converted, and some might also affect income available to common shareholders.</td></tr><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">D. They increase EPS or have no impact on it.</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Correct because anti-dilutive securities, when converted, would either increase the calculated EPS value or leave it unchanged. Including such securities would not show the potential negative effect on existing shareholders, which is the purpose of diluted EPS.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div style="background-color:#01506e; border-radius:0 0 8px 8px; color:white; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:white; margin:0 0 12px 0">Summary</h3><p style="margin:0">Diluted EPS is intended to show the worst-case scenario for earnings per share if all convertible securities were to be converted. Anti-dilutive securities are excluded from this calculation because:</p><ul style="margin:0 0 12px 0; padding-left:22px"><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Including them would make diluted EPS higher than basic EPS</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">This would contradict the purpose of diluted EPS, which is to show potential dilution</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">It would present a misleadingly favorable earnings picture to investors</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Accounting standards (like FASB ASC 260) specifically require their exclusion</li></ul><p style="margin:0">Key Takeaway: Anti-dilutive securities are excluded from diluted EPS calculations to ensure that the reported figure represents the maximum potential dilution that could occur, helping investors understand the worst-case scenario for their ownership stake.</p></div></div></div>`,
